        "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DVfDgF5D0wx/",
        "caption": "Interesting fact:\n\nTest flakiness — where tests randomly pass or fail without any code changes — is one of the most quietly expensive problems in software engineering, with Google reporting that 1 in 7 tests in their codebase exhibited flakiness at some point. A single flaky test can erode team trust so thoroughly that engineers begin routinely hitting \"re-run\" instead of investigating failures, effectively turning their CI/CD pipeline from a safety net into a formality. Microsoft research found that flaky tests waste an estimated developer hours in the thousands annually per large engineering org, as teams struggle to distinguish real bugs from phantom failures. The root causes are surprisingly mundane — race conditions, hardcoded timeouts, and test order dependencies account for the majority of flakiness, meaning most of the problem is solvable, yet it persists because fixing flaky tests rarely feels as urgent as shipping the next feature.",
